Round gen_r11 complete. The work in summary: I took the all-hands large-size mandate at my class's two traffic-bound sizes, restructured the chain step at L=64/128 into a single fused tile-resident sweep (x-stage-2 + map + z + y + x-stage-1, in place via a per-sweep plane-label permutation, with c stored in tile-consumption order after the first build proved the old layout's 1-KB-stride scatter defeats every prefetcher). It ships at L=128 (**-14% wall, -61% demand DRAM reads, 1.60x→1.75x vs MKL**, bit-identical output, all gates at r8's exact digits) and is measured out at L=64 (+2%), where LLC counters killed the old "L3-bandwidth-bound" theory — the regime boundary and the demand-vs-prefetch counter test are documented in my record as the transfer recipe for the L=100 owners. L=32/16 ship bit-identical for the fifth round; scored-cell layout tax raced as a wash.

One-sweep fused chain step (stage-split x across steps + tile-order c), ships at L=128: L=32 best 53.686 µs/transform batched (B=8) and 53.835 µs at B=1, L=128 12.19 ms/step-vol (from 14.6); rel L2 = 2.902e-16 single call, 1.338e-15 two-step fused gate (tol 3e-14), 3.328e-14 chain end — all PASS.
